# Observability & logging

## Wide events (canonical log lines)

Emit **one context-rich structured event per request per service hop** — not scattered log lines through the handler.

| Principle | Detail |
| --------- | ------ |
| **Single event** | Consolidate timing, status, business context at request completion (`finally`) |
| **High cardinality** | Include user IDs, request IDs — enables querying specific failures |
| **High dimensionality** | Many fields per event — answer questions you have not anticipated |
| **Business context** | Subscription tier, cart value, feature flags — not just HTTP status |
| **Environment** | Commit hash, service version, region, instance ID on every event |
| **Single logger** | One configured instance imported everywhere |
| **Two levels** | `info` and `error`; JSON only; no unstructured strings |

## Middleware pattern

Middleware owns timing, status, environment, emission. Handlers add business fields only.

## Anti-patterns

- Multiple `console.log` / logger calls per request
- Different logger instances per file
- Missing deployment context
- Logging technical details without user/business data
- Inconsistent field names across services

Propagate request IDs across service boundaries. Connect hops with the same correlation ID.

